  "title": "Quadratic Expressions",
  "body": " Quadratic Expressions   Quadratic Expressions   Introduction  In earlier modules, we worked with algebraic expressions. In this module we study a special kind of algebraic expression called a polynomial, and in particular quadratic expressions.  Let's consider the following example:  Brian visits a shop that sells sealed boxes. Each blue box contains 4 oranges and each red box contains 5 apples. Brian buys x blue boxes and y red boxes, and on his way home he also buys 8 bananas.     The x blue boxes hold 4x oranges.    the y red boxes contains 5y apples    The total number of fruits is      In this expression:   and are terms in the expression.     and are the unknown values called variables.     4 and 5 are the coefficients of and     8 is the constant term     Consider this other example:  A charity trust plans a rectangular prayer hall. Its length is 1 meter more than twice its length.   let the width be    The length will be     The area of the rectangle     is also an expression for the area of the rectangle. In this expression there is only one unknown value , thus it only has one variable.    Expressions such as , , and each contain a single variable. Each is called a polynomial with one variable or a univariate polynomial .  What are Polynomials  A polynomial is an expression in which the variable is raised only to whole-number powers ( ). For example, is a polynomial, but and are not.  The highest power of the variable is called the degree of the polynomial.    Polynomial  Highest power  Degree  Name      1  Linear      2  Quadratic      3  Cubic    Quadratic Expressions  A quadratic expression is a polynomial of degree 2. Its general form is , where , , and are numbers and .  If were 0, the term would vanish and we would be left with a linear expression. The numbers and may be zero, so and are both quadratic.    State the degree of each of the following polynomials and hence state whether its a quadratic expression.                           Formation of Quadratic Expressions  Quadratic expressions appear whenever two quantities that both depend on the same variable are multiplied together, or when a variable is squared. The product of two linear expressions is a quadratic expression.     Form a quadratic equation by multiplying the the following linear equations. and          Form a quadratic expression from the product of and .       We can also form quadratic expressions by squaring linear expressions     Form a quadratic equation by squaring the       Forming quadratic equations from Word Problems     A rectangular field has length metres and width metres. Form an expression for its area.          The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are cm and cm. Form an expression for the square of the hypotenuse.    From the Pythagorean theorem:         Form a quadratic expression for the product of and .  A rectangular room has length metres and breadth metres. Write an expression for its area.  Form an expression for the sum of the squares of two consecutive even numbers, and .  A trader sells items at a price of shillings each. Form an expression for the total income.  A rectangular field has length metres and width metres. Write its area, then find the area when .        Common Mistakes when forming Quadratic Expressions    Writing as . The correct expansion is .  Forgetting to multiply every term in the first bracket by every term in the second.  Sign errors, such as writing instead of .  Adding area expressions of different types without collecting like terms.       "
