  "title": "Qudratic Equations",
  "body": " Qudratic Equations   Quadratic Equations   Introduction  We have you studied quadratic expressions of the form When we equate this expression to zero we get a quadratic equation.  A quadratic equation in the variable is an equation of the form , where , , and are real numbers and .  For example, is a quadratic equation. Similarly, , , and are also quadratic equations.  Quadratic equations arise in several situations in the world around us and in different fields of mathematics.  Let us consider a few examples.     John and Kamau together have 45 marbles. Both of them lost 5 marbles each, and the product of the number of marbles they now have is 124.    Let the number of marbles John had be .  Then the number of marbles Kamau had is .  After both lose marbles each:    John has: marbles  Kamau has: marbles    Given that the product of their remaining marbles is :        A train travels a distance of 480 km at a uniform speed. If the speed had been 8 km\/h less, then it would have taken 3 hours more to cover the same distance.Find the speed of the train.    Let the original uniform speed of the train be .  The distance covered is .  Using the relationship :    Original time taken:  Time taken at reduced speed :    Given that the time difference is :          The area of a rectangular plot is . The length of the plot (in metres) is one more than twice its breadth. Find the length and breadth of the plot.    The product of two consecutive positive integers is . Find the integers.    Rohan’s mother is years older than him. The product of their ages (in years) years from now will be . Find Rohan’s present age.          Solving Quadratic Equations  A quadratic equation can be written in the standard form:   The values of that satisfy the equation are called the roots (or solutions) of the equation, which graphically respond to th x-intercepts of the parabola. A quadratic equation has at most two real roots.  There are three primary algebraic methods used to solve quadratic equations, along graphical inspection.   Solving Quadratic equations by Factorization   When a quadratic equations can be easily factored, this is often the fastest method. It relies on the Zero Product Property : If the product of two factors is zero, then at least one of the factors must be zero (i.e., if AB = 0, then A = 0 or B = 0).      Write the equation in standard form ( ).    Factor the quadratic expression into two linear binomials.    Set each binomial factor equal to zero.    Solve the resulting linear equations for .        Solve    Solve by factorization:  Find two numbers that multiply to and add to : these are and .   Set each factor equal to zero and solve for :        Solve for , in    Rewrite the equation in standard form :   Factorise by finding two numbers that multiply to and add to (these are and ):   Set each binomial factor equal to zero and solve for :        The length of a rectangular plot of land is longer than its width. If the total area of the plot is , find its dimensions.    Let the width of the rectangular plot be . Then the length is .  Using the area formula :   Factorise by finding two numbers that multiply to and add to (these are and ):   Set each factor equal to zero:   Since width must be positive, we reject . Thus, the width is and the length is .    Solving Quadratic equations by completing the square  This method transforms any quadratic equation into a perfect square quadratic equation equal to a constant, making it easy to solve by taking square roots.  To solve for the roots of a quadratic equation by completing square:      Ensure the leading coefficient (divide the entire equation by if necessary).    Move the constant term to the right side of the equation.    Add to both sides to complete the square on the left.    Express the left side as a squared binomial and simplify the right side.    Take the square root of both sides and solve for .         Solve by completing the square:    Solve by completing the square:  Move the constant term to the right side of the equation:   Add to both sides to complete the square:   Take the square root of both sides and solve for :        A school plans to construct a rectangular playground whose length is longer than its width. If the total area of the playground must be , find the dimensions of the playground using the completing the square method.    Let the width of the rectangular playground be . Then the length is .  Using the area formula :   Complete the square by adding to both sides:   Take the square root of both sides and solve for :   Since physical width must be positive, we reject . Thus, the width of the playground is and the length is .    Solving Quadratic equations Using quadratic formula  The universal method that works for any quadratic equation,whether it can be easily factored or not. It is derived from completing the square. The formula is:   The term inside the square root, , is called the Discriminant , which determines the nature of the roots:    : Two distinct real roots.  : One repeated real root (a perfect square).  : Two complex (non-real) roots.     Derivation of the quadratic formula   The quadratic formula can be universally derived for any quadratic equation by applying the method of completing the square to the standard form.   The General Quadratic Equation   Start with the standard form of a quadratic equation, where :    Step-by-Step Derivation       Make the leading coefficient equal to 1: Divide every term in the equation by :      Isolate the variable terms: Move the constant term to the right-hand side of the equation:      Complete the square on the left side: Take the coefficient of , which is , divide it by to get , and square it to get .  Add this value to both sides of the equation:      Simplify both sides: Express the left side as a squared binomial and combine the fractions on the right side using the common denominator :      Take the square root of both sides: Apply the square root property, including both the positive and negative roots ( ):   Simplify the denominator on the right side since :      Isolate : Subtract from both sides and combine the terms under the common denominator:       Result   This completes the derivation, yielding the universal Quadratic Formula:      Solve    Solve using the quadratic formula:  Identify the coefficients , , and .  Substitute these values directly into :   Solve for both cases:        Solve by quadratic formula:    Solve using the quadratic formula:  Identify the coefficients , , and .  Substitute these values directly into :   Solve for both cases:          Solve by factorization.    Solve by completing the square.    Solve using the quadratic formula.    Solve by factorization.    Solve by completing the square.    Solve using the quadratic formula.    The length of a rectangular lawn is longer than its width. If the area of the lawn is , calculate its length and width by factorization.    The product of two consecutive positive integers is . Set up a quadratic equation and solve it by completing the square to find the two integers.    A ball is kicked vertically upwards into the air. Its height (in metres) after seconds is given by . Find the time(s) when the ball reaches a height of using the quadratic formula.    The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is . The other two sides are and . Use Pythagoras' Theorem to form a quadratic equation and solve for .           "
